"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,487\nEpisode 18\n\n3\n00:00:07,567 --> 00:00:09,161\nTransfer\n\n4\n00:00:11,961 --> 00:00:14,820\nA doctor must destroy a tumor with rays.\n\n5\n00:00:15,151 --> 00:00:19,948\nA ray strong enough to kill the tumor will also destroy the healthy tissue around it.\n\n6\n00:00:20,442 --> 00:00:26,062\nEarlier, people had read a story: a general captures a fortress by splitting his army,\n\n7\n00:00:26,104 --> 00:00:29,198\nand sending small groups down many roads at once.\n\n8\n00:00:29,616 --> 00:00:30,978\nMost did not use it.\n\n9\n00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:32,712\nTold the story might help,\n\n10\n00:00:32,949 --> 00:00:35,492\nabout three in four solved the problem.\n\n11\n00:00:35,662 --> 00:00:39,422\nThat is transfer: using what you learned in a new situation.\n\n12\n00:00:39,730 --> 00:00:41,011\nThe knowledge was there.\n\n13\n00:00:41,435 --> 00:00:43,805\nWhat failed was noticing that it applied.\n\n14\n00:00:44,247 --> 00:00:46,882\nStudents meet the same idea in new clothing,\n\n15\n00:00:46,902 --> 00:00:48,518\nand do not recognize it.\n\n16\n00:00:48,901 --> 00:00:51,186\nNear transfer, to similar situations,\n\n17\n00:00:51,206 --> 00:00:52,015\nis common.\n\n18\n00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:54,800\nFar transfer, to very different ones,\n\n19\n00:00:54,843 --> 00:00:56,511\nis rare and hard won.\n\n20\n00:00:56,681 --> 00:00:59,481\nThis is also why brain training disappoints.\n\n21\n00:00:59,861 --> 00:01:03,220\nIn a six week study of more than eleven thousand adults,\n\n22\n00:01:03,453 --> 00:01:06,165\npeople improved on every task they practiced,\n\n23\n00:01:06,364 --> 00:01:09,318\nbut showed no gains on untrained tasks,\n\n24\n00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:11,476\neven closely related ones.\n\n25\n00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:14,653\nA later review reached a similar conclusion.\n\n26\n00:01:15,056 --> 00:01:17,706\nPractice makes you better at what you practice.\n\n27\n00:01:17,876 --> 00:01:19,450\nWhat helps is comparison.\n\n28\n00:01:19,825 --> 00:01:23,992\nWhen people read two analogous stories and described what they had in common,\n\n29\n00:01:24,274 --> 00:01:26,930\nthey were much more likely to solve the new problem.\n\n30\n00:01:27,412 --> 00:01:29,276\nThey had built a general schema,\n\n31\n00:01:29,482 --> 00:01:31,543\nnot just a memory of one story.\n\n32\n00:01:32,071 --> 00:01:38,605\nA meta-analysis of fifty seven experiments found that case comparison reliably improved learning,\n\n33\n00:01:38,942 --> 00:01:41,776\nespecially when learners looked for similarities,\n\n34\n00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:43,427\nand then got the principle.\n\n35\n00:01:43,597 --> 00:01:44,391\nFor teaching.\n\n36\n00:01:44,812 --> 00:01:49,545\nTeach with two or more examples that differ on the surface but share the deep structure.\n\n37\n00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:52,718\nAsk students what the examples have in common.\n\n38\n00:01:53,048 --> 00:01:55,224\nName the principle after they compare.\n\n39\n00:01:55,579 --> 00:02:00,288\nMix problem types, so students must recognize which idea applies.\n\n40\n00:02:00,619 --> 00:02:01,936\nAnd tell them plainly: this idea will show up again,\n\n41\n00:02:02,224 --> 00:02:05,750\nin places that look different.\n\n42\n00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:07,169\nAcross this series: attention,\n\n43\n00:02:07,382 --> 00:02:09,839\nmemory, retrieval, spacing, sleep,\n\n44\n00:02:09,859 --> 00:02:11,885\nemotion, and each other.\n\n45\n00:02:12,337 --> 00:02:14,232\nThe brain learns what it does.\n\n46\n00:02:14,569 --> 00:02:15,956\nDesign what students do,\n\n47\n00:02:15,976 --> 00:02:17,966\nand you design what they learn.\n\n"
